::MCR_U03_L03_GP_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L03_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - Extension of MLA.MCR.DL.2 and MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Set A has median 74 and Set B has median 82. What does this compare?
{
=A. center#Correct. Median describes a typical or center value.
~B. variability#Variability describes spread, not typical value.
~C. outlier#An outlier is an unusual value, not the median.
~D. sample size#Sample size is the number of observations.
}
::MCR_U03_L03_GP_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L03_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - Extension of MLA.MCR.DL.2 and MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Set A range is 8 and Set B range is 30. Which set is more spread out?
{
~A. Set A#Set A has the smaller range, so it is less spread out.
=B. Set B#Correct. The larger range shows greater spread.
~C. Both are equal#The ranges are different, so spread is not equal.
~D. Cannot compare#The range values provide enough information to compare spread.
}
::MCR_U03_L03_GP_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L03_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - Extension of MLA.MCR.DL.2 and MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Which comparison is strongest?
{
~A. Set A is better.#This is too vague and does not use data.
~B. Set B has numbers.#This does not explain a comparison.
=C. Set A has a higher median but Set B has a larger range.#Correct. This compares both center and spread.
~D. The graph looks nice.#Visual appearance is not data evidence.
}
::MCR_U03_L03_GP_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L03_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - Extension of MLA.MCR.DL.2 and MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
A data set is 10, 11, 12, 13, 60. What is 60?
{
~A. median#The median is the middle value, not 60.
~B. range#Range is a difference, not a data value.
~C. cluster#The cluster is around 10 to 13.
=D. outlier#Correct. The value 60 is far away from the rest of the values.
}
::MCR_U03_L03_GP_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L03_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only - Extension of MLA.MCR.DL.2 and MLA.MCR.DL.4
Question:
Two classes have the same median, but Class B has a much larger range. What is true?
{
~A. Class B has a higher median.#The medians are stated to be the same.
=B. Class B is less consistent.#Correct. A larger range means the scores are more spread out, so the class is less consistent.
~C. Class A has an outlier for sure.#A smaller range does not prove an outlier.
~D. No comparison can be made.#The range allows a comparison of variability.
}
